{"question": "A 52-year-old man with a history of gastric cancer that was treated with subtotal gastrectomy dies in a motor vehicle collision. At autopsy, examination of the spinal cord shows unilateral atrophy of the neurons in the area indicated by the arrow. Neurological examination of the patient when he was still alive would most likely have shown which of the following findings?", "options": {"A": "Decreased sense of temperature in the ipsilateral arm", "B": "Decreased strength of the contralateral leg", "C": "Decreased vibratory sense in the ipsilateral arm", "D": "Decreased positional sense in the ipsilateral leg", "E": "Decreased sense of crude touch in the contralateral arm"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Neurology", "Oncology", "Surgery", "Traumatology", "Pathology"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Pathology"], "question_analyses": {"Neurology": "The medical scenario describes a 52-year-old man who had a history of gastric cancer treated with a subtotal gastrectomy and subsequently died in a motor vehicle collision. At autopsy, the spinal cord examination revealed unilateral atrophy of the neurons in a specific area, indicated by an arrow. This finding suggests a condition affecting the spinal cord, specifically a lesion that has led to neuronal loss on one side.\n\n### Interpretation of the Condition:\nThe unilateral atrophy of the neurons in the spinal cord is indicative of a condition known as **spinal cord hemisection** or **Brown-S\u00e9quard syndrome**. This syndrome results from a lesion that disrupts the spinal cord on one side, leading to a characteristic pattern of neurological deficits. The key features of Brown-S\u00e9quard syndrome include:\n\n1. **Contralateral Sensory Loss**: Loss of pain and temperature sensation on the same side as the lesion.\n2. ** Ipsilateral Motor Loss**: Loss of motor function on the same side as the lesion.\n3. ** Ipsilateral Deep Sensory Loss**: Loss of proprioception and fine tou